# Add visual goldens and complete keyboard-only editor journeys

## Scope and existing evidence

Introduce reproducible notebook PNG/PDF goldens and the keyboard
navigation/refusal flow for every currently implemented page-editor control.

This is unfinished implementation work. The following original design
records remain accepted theoretical deliverables and are not reopened:

- ATR-P8-003 — archived release design
- ATR-P8-004 — archived release design

## Trusted keyboard-only native profile journey (partial) — 2026-11-03

A real headless Firefox/WebDriver BiDi test uses only trusted Tab, Shift+Tab,
ArrowDown, Enter, Ctrl+A and character keys to edit two live localhost
profiles. It does not invoke DOM click, focus, value assignment or synthetic
input events to make the profile changes. The browser persists a selected
date style, placement and numeric date in a native notebook JSON file and
an exact handwriting seed in a separate native Rust handwriting JSON file.

After unsaved keyboard edits, the test reopens each file and checks the
original values and unchanged disk bytes. Invalid February 30 and a seed
above unsigned 64-bit range trigger visible refusal and preserve both files.

Focused regression:

```sh
node --test \
  --test-name-pattern='Firefox trusted keyboard saves and reopens' \
  tests/browser/workspace/adapter-inbound/runtime-frame-ancestors.mjs
```

This establishes a real keyboard-only save/reopen subset. It does not provide
approved ruled/squared raster goldens or verify every currently implemented
page-editor control, so this record remains open.

## Disposable Firefox process-group cleanup (partial) — 2026-11-04

The Linux BiDi launcher now tracks each owned detached Firefox group, kills
surviving helpers when the launcher exits, and reaps remaining owned groups
when the test worker exits normally. Bounded shutdown also reaps a group when
a child exit is observable only through its exit code, even if the event was
missed. Linux subprocess fixtures verify surviving helpers are terminated
without signalling a shared process group; eight successive focused runs and
two actual BiDi journeys passed.

`pnpm run test:frontend` now uses one test-file worker at a time so multiple
resource-intensive Firefox journeys do not contend on the workstation.
This is fixture/test-runner reliability evidence, not an approved PNG/PDF
golden or proof of every keyboard-only control. The subsequent
source-authority review restored 70/70 browser privacy and 43/43 native
authority tests, including checks that reject an extra subprocess in the
opt-in print CLI. These audits do not substitute for the still-open visual
goldens and full keyboard journeys.

## Abrupt worker termination fixture cleanup (partial) — 2026-11-04

An independent Linux watchdog now observes each test-owned detached Firefox
fixture over a private pipe. When its test worker is killed with SIGKILL,
the watchdog sees pipe closure and kills only that fixture process group;
normal launcher exit retains the existing cleanup observer. Isolated Node
fixtures test both pipe closure and a real SIGKILL of the parent worker,
including the detached child reaching an exited or zombie state, without
signalling an unrelated browser session. A real BiDi browser journey remains
necessary alongside these synthetic process fixtures. This does not change
production browser lifecycle guarantees or complete the still-open visual
goldens and keyboard-only control coverage.

## Acceptance decision

Open. The finite implementation described above is not established by
the original theoretical design acceptance. This follow-up must be
evaluated separately against its practical or theoretical scope.
